<Base
  title="Laying the first stone: honor and glory"
  description="Iliad Book 1: why Achilles withdraws, what a prize is worth, and the promise Zeus makes to Thetis. Samuel Butler’s translation, 1898."
>
  <LessonChrome slug="the-wrath">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Laying the first stone: honor and glory</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we add honor to the foundation. Homer, <em>Iliad</em>, Book 1, is the
        text, included for reference.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Honor is the first layer of the man. The prologue is the ground under it. No layer of character sits under this lesson.</li>
        <li>In the <em>Iliad</em>, that honor is a prize other people can see, and Achilles’ anger is what follows when Agamemnon takes it.</li>
        <li>The quarrel shows what the prize builds: a reason to fight, and a public name for a theft.</li>
        <li>It also shows where honor alone fails. No one both men will obey can settle it, and the promise that restores it is paid in other men’s deaths.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we add honor to the foundation.</p>
      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Setting the scene</h3>
      <p>
        Let’s begin with the person this course is building. He is assembled one
        layer at a time, in the order Greek society itself grows more complex. A
        household, a city, a court, an examined mind, and practical wisdom all come
        later. They are meant to stand on this layer. They are not in the book you
        are about to read.
      </p>
      <p>
        This first layer is the warrior. The world of the poem is a camp of kings
        at Troy. Worth is public: a prize, a place in the assembly, a song after
        death. Mycenae is the citadel the later tradition gave to Agamemnon. Its
        Lion Gate belongs to the UNESCO listing “Archaeological Sites of Mycenae
        and Tiryns,” with a relieving triangle over the lintel. The gold mask
        Schliemann called Agamemnon’s is from Grave Circle A, about 1550–1500 BC,
        and it is centuries older than the Trojan War as that later tradition told
        it. The name on the mask is his, not Homer’s. You do not need the gate or
        the mask. You need Book 1.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>Let’s begin with the camp</h3>
      <p>
        You are in the Greek camp at Troy, in a war that has already gone on for
        years. The poem does not start with Helen leaving Sparta. It starts by
        telling you what it is about, and then it shows you that thing happening.
      </p>
      <p>
        The English below is Samuel Butler’s translation of 1898. He calls Zeus
        Jove, Hera Juno, and Athena Minerva. When the reader says Jove, read Zeus.
        The line numbers used in this lesson are the Greek numbers.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>The first sentence is the whole story</h3>
      <p>
        Notice the order of the facts in the opening. The goddess is asked to sing
        the anger of Achilles, son of Peleus. That anger brought countless ills on
        the Greeks. It sent souls to Hades and left bodies for dogs and vultures.
        The poet says the counsels of Zeus were fulfilled in this. He dates it from
        the day Agamemnon, “king of men,” and Achilles fell out with one another.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first Greek word is μῆνιν, <em>mēnin</em>, “anger,” the thing the
        goddess is asked to sing. The noun is μῆνις, <em>mēnis</em>. Keep it. Two
        other words belong beside it. <em>Timē</em> is honour as other people award
        it: a prize, a share, a place everyone can see. <em>Kleos</em> is the glory
        a song can keep after the man is dead. Book 1 is a fight about <em>timē</em>.
        Someone is about to take a prize back.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>A priest, a king, and nine days of arrows</h3>
      <p>
        Apollo is angry because Agamemnon has dishonoured Chryses, his priest.
        Chryses comes to the ships with a ransom, and with the sceptre and wreath
        of the god, and asks for his daughter, Chryseis. He wishes the Greeks
        success. The army, with one voice, wants the ransom accepted. Agamemnon
        does not. He tells the old man the god’s emblems will profit him nothing,
        and that the girl will grow old in his house at Argos.
      </p>
      <p>
        Chryses prays along the shore. Apollo hears, and for nine days his arrows
        kill mules, dogs, and then men. On the tenth day Achilles calls an assembly.
        The poem says Hera moved him to it, because she pitied the dying. Achilles
        says they must learn why Apollo is angry, or sail home. Calchas, the seer,
        will speak only under Achilles’ protection, because he is about to blame
        the king. Achilles swears no one will touch the seer while he lives. Calchas
        then says the plague ends when Agamemnon returns the girl, without payment,
        and with a holy offering.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>The prize is the honour</h3>
      <p>
        Agamemnon agrees to give Chryseis back, because he wants the army alive,
        and he demands another prize in her place. The shares from sacked towns
        have already been given out. Achilles says there is nothing left in common,
        and that if Troy ever falls they will repay the king. Agamemnon refuses to
        be the only man who loses a prize. He names the one he will take: Briseis,
        from Achilles’ own tent, so that Achilles learns which of them is stronger,
        and so that no one else will claim to be his equal.
      </p>
      <p>
        Achilles’ answer is an account of why he is there. The Trojans have not
        raided his cattle or cut his fields at Phthia. He came for Agamemnon and
        for Menelaus. The prize he holds was given by the army, for the fighting
        he did. When the king takes it, he takes the public mark of that work.
        Achilles says he will not stay, dishonoured, to gather wealth for someone
        else. His hand goes to his sword. Athena, sent by Hera, and visible only to
        him, tells him to put it back. He may speak. Later, she says, gifts three
        times as splendid will come because of this insult. He obeys, and the sword
        goes back into the scabbard.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then he swears on the sceptre. It is a dead branch. It will never leaf
        again. Just as surely, when Hector is killing the Greeks, they will look
        for Achilles and will not find him. He sits. Nestor tries to part them.
        Neither man yields. Heralds come for Briseis. Achilles is courteous to
        them; his quarrel is with the king. Briseis goes, unwilling. He walks alone
        by the sea and calls his mother.
      </p>

      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>A short life, and a nod</h3>
      <p>
        Thetis is a goddess. Achilles is not. He says so: he was born to a short
        life, and Zeus might have made that short life glorious. Agamemnon has
        robbed him of the prize. Thetis tells him to stay out of the fighting. In
        twelve days, when Zeus is back from the Ethiopians, she will ask. She does.
        She kneels, a suppliant, and asks Zeus to let the Trojans win until the
        Greeks give her son his due. Zeus is silent, because Hera will be angry.
        Then he nods. He says he does not recall a word once he has nodded. That
        nod is the “counsels of Jove” the opening lines promised. The deaths those
        lines named are how the promise will be kept.
      </p>
      <p>
        The book ends in heaven, at a feast, with Hera suspicious and Hephaestus
        smoothing it over. Down at the ships, the best fighter is out of the war
        on purpose.
      </p>
      <hr class="ornament" />
      <h3>What honor builds, and where it fails alone</h3>
      <p>
        Notice what the prize has done, before you judge the quarrel. Achilles is
        not at Troy because his own fields were cut. He says so. He came for
        Agamemnon and for Menelaus, and the army gave him Briseis as the mark of
        the fighting he did. That is what honor builds in this book: a reason to
        take up someone else’s war, and a share everyone can point to. When the
        king takes the share back, Achilles can name the theft. He can also put
        the sword away and withhold the work. Rank is not the only voice in the
        camp.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now notice what honor cannot do by itself. Nestor asks both men to yield.
        Neither does. There is no court in Book 1 above a king and the best
        fighter. Athena stops the sword. She does not return the prize. Zeus nods,
        and the way he keeps Achilles’ honour is to let the Trojans win until the
        Greeks pay it back. The opening has already named the price: souls to
        Hades, and bodies for dogs and vultures. Honor can say what was taken. Left
        alone, it cannot stop the deaths that restoring it will cost.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you work in a profession, you already know a plainer version of the
        same limit. A case, a credit, a place your name stood: rank can take the
        visible share back, and the person who was doing the work can withhold the
        work. The poem’s point is older than the office. A prize can hold a person
        to a task, and a prize alone cannot settle the quarrel it starts. The
        later layers of this course exist for that gap. Book 1 stops before they
        arrive. The best fighter is out of the war on purpose, and the dying the
        nod promised has not started.
      </p>
    </div>

