    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="word-day">
      <h2 id="word-day">Word of the day</h2>
      <p class="word-greek" lang="grc">μῆνις</p>
      <p>
        <em>mēnis</em>, anger. In the first line it is μῆνιν, the object of “sing.”
        Say it once, aloud: <em>mēnin aeide thea</em>. Then find “anger” in the
        first sentence of the reader.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Latin word for anger is <em>ira</em>. Homer’s word is the Greek one.
        You do not need a textbook for this. A grammar book can wait.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Introspection
      slug="the-wrath"
      prompts={[
        "Agamemnon takes Briseis so that Achilles will see the king is stronger. Think of a time someone with more rank took back credit, a role or a reward you had earned. What did you do next, and what would you do now?",
        "Agamemnon refused to free Chryseis even when the army wanted the ransom accepted, and nine days of arrows followed. Is there something you are holding onto out of pride while the people around you pay for it?",
        "Nestor asks both men to yield, and neither does. In a quarrel you are in now, at work or at home, what would yielding first actually cost you? What would it gain the people who depend on you?",
