      ]}
    />

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Honor is the foundation. The prologue is the ground of civilization under it. There is no layer of the man under this lesson.</li>
        <li>
          It builds a public worth. <em>Timē</em> is the prize the army gave.
          <em>Kleos</em> is the glory a song can keep. <em>Mēnis</em>, the first
          word of the poem, is the anger when the prize is taken.
        </li>
        <li>
          It also builds a check on rank. Achilles can name the theft and withhold
          the fighting. The sword goes back into the scabbard because Athena tells
          him later gifts will come.
        </li>
        <li>
          It fails where a settlement is needed. Nestor is not obeyed. A goddess,
          not a law, stops the sword. The nod that restores the honour is paid in
          other men’s lives.
        </li>
        <li>
          Nothing in the man, yet, can catch that failure. A household, a citizen’s city, a court, an examined mind, and
          practical wisdom are the layers still to be laid. They stand on this one.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        Book 1 lays the stone. Honor gets Achilles to Troy and gives him a name
        for what Agamemnon took. The same honor, with nothing above it, sends him
        out of the war and leaves the deaths to Zeus’s nod. That is the foundation,
        and that is its limit.
      </p>
    </section>

